# 3. DEEP CANDLE PSYCHOLOGY ENGINE

For every completed candle, calculate a standardized set of characteristics.

### 3.1 Candle measurements

Calculate:

- Total candle range.
- Real body size.
- Upper wick size.
- Lower wick size.
- Body-to-range percentage.
- Upper wick percentage.
- Lower wick percentage.
- Close location within candle range.
- Body size relative to ATR.
- Total range relative to ATR.
- Gap from previous candle close.
- Current candle overlap with previous candles.
- Candle direction.
- Consecutive candle direction count.
- Rolling volatility.
- Available volume relative to a rolling average.

Use consistent normalization so these measurements work across XAUUSD, Forex pairs and other supported symbols.

Handle zero-range candles and invalid OHLC values safely.

### 3.2 Candle classification

Classify candles into meaningful categories, such as:

- Strong Bullish.
- Strong Bearish.
- Weak Bullish.
- Weak Bearish.
- Bullish Rejection.
- Bearish Rejection.
- Bullish Engulfing.
- Bearish Engulfing.
- Indecision / Doji.
- Inside Bar.
- Outside Bar.
- High-Volatility Impulse.
- Low-Volatility Compression.
- Possible Exhaustion.
- Failed Breakout / Reclaim.
- Neutral.

A candle may have multiple compatible attributes.

Do not force every candle into exactly one exclusive pattern.

### 3.3 Initial threshold examples

Use configurable, testable starting thresholds:

- Strong body: approximately 65–70% of candle range.
- Significant rejection wick: approximately 45–55% of total range.
- Meaningful impulse body: approximately 0.8–1.2 ATR.
- Strong range expansion: approximately 1.3–1.8 ATR.
- Doji/indecision body: approximately 10–15% of range.

These are experimental starting values, not universally proven settings.

Do not make all conditions mandatory.

Keep thresholds moderately permissive and validate their effects using historical datasets.

