# 7. WATERFALL ENTRY STRATEGIES

Do not enter automatically after every large candle.

Implement distinct setup types.

## Setup A — Early Momentum Confirmation

Detect the initial meaningful displacement and breakout.

Requirements may include:

- Strong directional impulse.
- Relevant structure break.
- Close near candle extreme.
- Sufficient room before nearby opposing structure.
- Acceptable risk/reward based on available prices.

Issue a candidate at the initial impulse and confirm only when its required conditions are satisfied.

Avoid waiting for five or six candles when an earlier objectively valid setup already exists.

## Setup B — First Pullback Entry

After strong directional momentum:

- Detect first meaningful counter-direction retracement.
- Evaluate pullback depth.
- Identify nearby FVG, OB or breakout level if available.
- Detect continuation confirmation.
- Generate a new entry candidate at the appropriate time.

This should be one of the preferred waterfall continuation setup types.

## Setup C — Compression Breakout

After strong momentum:

- Detect temporary consolidation.
- Identify compression range.
- Recognize renewed directional breakout.
- Confirm the breakout without future information.
- Generate a continuation setup.

## Setup D — Momentum Retest

When a strong impulse breaks a relevant level:

- Mark the broken level.
- Observe a retest.
- Analyze rejection or continuation behavior.
- Generate an eligible setup when confirmed.

## Setup E — Shallow Continuation

Some strong momentum trends provide little retracement.

Allow a carefully defined continuation entry based on a new completed-candle confirmation and appropriate risk conditions.

Do not require an FVG, liquidity sweep, Order Block and MSS simultaneously.

Use different validation rules for different setup types.

